  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nShortwaves in the upper level southwest flow will continue to \nbring an occasional low chance for showers and thunderstorms \n(10-30% chance) through the end of the work week. SPC has issued a\nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for much of Montana today. The main\nthreat with any thunderstorms will be strong wind gusts of 60+\nmph.\n\nBetween a low northwest of the region and a high southeast of the\nregion, high temperatures will vary from upper 70s to low 80s \nover the west to near 90 over the east today through at least\nFriday. \n\nUnder the current pattern, winds will occasionally be breezy,\nespecially over the mountains and foothills. Gusts of 20 to 30 \nmph are expected over the plains and 35 to 50 mph gusts are\nexpected over the mountains and foothills at times today and\nThursday. With the mostly dry conditions, fire weather concerns\nare elevated. Given the prolonged period of near to above normal\ntemperatures and breezy conditions, western and central areas of\nthe CWA are of the most concern Thursday and a Fire Weather Watch\nhas been issued. Locations in the higher terrain recently \nreceived up to 1\" of precip, and thus fire concerns decrease with\nelevation.\n\nFor the weekend into early next week, there is more uncertainty in\nthe placement of the upper low, how frequently shortwaves will\nmove through the flow, and when the low will begin to approach the\nregion. At this time, the most likely scenario is for the current\npattern to continue into at least early Sunday. This will continue\nto bring a low chance for showers and thunderstorms, hot\ntemperatures over the east, and more seasonable temperatures over\nthe west. Early next week, once the system begins to approach,\ntemperatures will cool and the chance for precipitation will\nincrease. Archer",
